  "text": "When the screen isn't SQL, it's pandas. The same ideas come back as a data frame, and so do the questions. There are thirty you should be ready for. Seventeen of them hide a trap, so we'll take those slowly. The other thirteen get one answer each, in a rapid-fire round. Most screens start by asking you to pick out some rows.\n\nQuestion one. What's the difference between lock and eye lock? Lock picks by label, the names on the index and the columns. Eye lock picks by position, counting from zero. On a fresh frame they agree. Lock at label three and eye lock at row three both find fifty. Now keep only the North rows. Their labels are two and four. Eye lock zero still works: it's the first row, a hundred and twenty. Lock zero fails with a key error, because no row is labelled zero.\n\nQuestion two. How does boolean filtering work? A comparison on a column gives a column of trues and falses, called a mask. Combine two masks with an ampersand, and pass it to lock. The two North orders over a hundred come back. The trap is the brackets. Leave them off, and the ampersand runs first, on a hundred and the region column, so Python stops with a type error. And the word and fails too: the truth value of a Series is ambiguous. Then comes the one every pandas screen asks.",
